              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your 18-year-old male patient, admitted on 20/06/2009 with a two-day history of right lower quadrant pain, nausea, and low-grade fever, clinically consistent with acute appendicitis. On admission, physical examination revealed marked rebound tenderness at McBurney's point. Blood tests confirmed leukocytosis, and abdominal ultrasound supported the diagnosis of acute appendicitis. The patient was resuscitated with intravenous fluids and commenced on prophylactic antibiotics. He subsequently underwent laparoscopic emergency appendectomy on 20/06/2009 without intraoperative complications. Histopathology confirmed acute appendicitis. Postoperative recovery was uneventful; wound care was maintained and vital signs remained stable throughout. The patient was mobilised early and discharged home on 22/06/2009 in good general condition. Please ensure appropriate outpatient follow-up and wound review. Do not hesitate to contact me should any concerns arise.</div>"
            },
